# Environment contract for ebook-reader (single file, repo root).
# Copy to `.env` and fill EVERY variable — both apps validate on startup and
# refuse to run if any of these are missing or malformed.

# --- Web (Vite) -----------------------------------------------------------
# Must be prefixed VITE_ to be exposed to the client. Loaded from this root
# file via Vite's envDir (see apps/web/vite.config.ts).
# In development this is the web dev server's own /atrium-api, which it proxies
# to the API (D54), so the page and the API share an origin as they do in the
# deploy. The path prefix is kept: apiUrl() in api-client.ts preserves it.
VITE_API_URL=http://localhost:5173/atrium-api

# The sub-path the web app is served under. The deploy sets it itself; locally
# it makes the dev server match, so Ward's redirect back to /atrium/ lands in
# the app. Open http://localhost:5173/atrium/.
BASE_PATH=/atrium/

# --- API (Fastify) --------------------------------------------------------
PORT=3001
HOST=0.0.0.0

# --- Ward (the estate's identity service) ---------------------------------
# Atrium has no accounts, no passwords and no login page of its own. Identity
# is Ward's: the browser holds a `ward_session` cookie for the whole origin,
# and atrium validates it on every request. There is nothing to seed.
#
# All three are REQUIRED — the API refuses to boot without them, because a
# missing one is a total outage (every request 503s) rather than a degraded
# mode, and failing at startup is far cheaper to diagnose than failing on
# somebody's first page load.

# Ward's public origin. Also the exact `iss` every access token must carry.
# Bare origin, no trailing slash. Locally that is Ward's container in
# ../wzd_auth/infrastructure/local; the deploy passes https://gandolh.ro. The web
# dev server proxies /ward and /ward-api here (D54).
WARD_PUBLIC_ORIGIN=http://localhost:8792

# Ward's prefix behind Caddy. `/ward-api` in this estate.
#
# No default, deliberately: an empty value resolves the JWKS to
# `<origin>/.well-known/jwks.json`, a path nothing serves — which would make
# atrium reject every token, with a clean log, on the deploy that shipped it.
WARD_API_BASE_PATH=/ward-api

# Atrium's own Ward service key. A SECRET — server-side only, never a VITE_
# variable, never logged.
#
# Locally, the Ward container's seed.mjs writes it into .env for you. Otherwise
# issue it in Ward's console: the atrium app's page, "Service keys". It is
# shown ONCE and cannot be read back (Ward stores only a digest). If it is
# lost, issue a new one and revoke the old one — which is also the rotation
# procedure, and atrium may hold several live keys at once so a rotation needs
# no window where it has none.
WARD_APP_KEY=wak_replace_me

MAX_UPLOAD_MB=50

# Ceiling on a library conversion job (brief 34 / D34), in milliseconds.
# 24 hours. That is a last-resort reaper, not a UX guard: nobody waits at the
# screen for a conversion — the book's row says "running", the client polls, and
# the person walks away. This number exists only so a wedged `ebook-convert`
# can't pin a CPU forever. The answer to "this is taking too long" is the Cancel
# button, not a shorter ceiling.
CONVERT_JOB_TIMEOUT_MS=86400000

# Optional (brief 22): base URL of the Gutendex instance the catalog proxy
# queries. Defaults to the public community instance when unset; override to
# point at a self-hosted mirror. Not required — leave unset for normal use.
# GUTENDEX_BASE_URL=https://gutendex.com

# Optional LaTeX limits (brief 38), not part of the required contract above.
# Each has a sensible default — set one only to tune it.
#
# LATEX_TIMEOUT_MS is the compile route's outer wall-clock backstop, NOT the
# real guard: the typesetting engine is a pure function with a deterministic
# step budget (D38), and that budget is what stops a runaway macro — at the
# same point on every machine. This timer only covers a wedge outside the
# engine's step loop. Two minutes is generous on purpose; a shorter ceiling
# would not add safety, it would just fail slow-but-valid documents on a slow
# machine, non-deterministically.
# LATEX_TIMEOUT_MS=120000
#
# Cap on the compiled PDF one compile may produce. A tiny document can emit a
# huge output, so the project cap below does not imply this one.
# LATEX_MAX_OUTPUT_MB=25
#
# Cap on a project's whole working tree — sources plus every uploaded figure
# and .bib. MAX_UPLOAD_MB bounds a single file; this bounds their sum.
# LATEX_MAX_PROJECT_MB=50

# Optional storage-root overrides, not part of the required contract above.
# Each defaults to a path under apps/api/ when unset. Redirect all five
# together to point a test/deploy at a scratch storage root, so a scratch
# database and scratch files move as one — redirecting only LIBRARY_DATA_DIR
# still leaves LIBRARY_FILES_DIR/THUMBNAILS_DIR pointed at the real files,
# which is how a verification run destroyed a real book on 2026-08-25.
# LIBRARY_DATA_DIR=/tmp/atrium-scratch/data
# LIBRARY_FILES_DIR=/tmp/atrium-scratch/library
# THUMBNAILS_DIR=/tmp/atrium-scratch/thumbnails
# LaTeX project working trees (drafts) and published version artifacts
# (brief 38). Overridable for the same reason as the three above — a root that
# can't be redirected can't be tested destructively (D39).
# LATEX_PROJECTS_DIR=/tmp/atrium-scratch/latex
# DOCUMENT_VERSIONS_DIR=/tmp/atrium-scratch/versions
